WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.139
Logs are sensitive operational data.

2
00:00:02.139 --> 00:00:14.235
Even well-designed logs can reveal system behavior, customer activity,
administrative actions, support cases, security signals, internal service
names, environment details, and privacy-relevant metadata.

3
00:00:14.235 --> 00:00:16.441
Access should be based on role and need.

4
00:00:16.441 --> 00:00:25.931
Developers, support staff, security analysts, administrators, auditors,
and vendors may need different views, different retention, and different
approval paths.

5
00:00:25.931 --> 00:00:28.538
Production log access deserves special care.

6
00:00:28.538 --> 00:00:32.481
Broad access can turn logs into a workaround for data access controls.

7
00:00:32.481 --> 00:00:41.102
A person who cannot query production databases directly may still learn
sensitive facts from logs if events include too much detail or access is
too open.

8
00:00:41.102 --> 00:00:51.395
Role-based access, reviewable permissions, just-in-time access,
environment separation, and audit trails for log access can reduce that
risk while still supporting operations.

9
00:00:51.395 --> 00:00:57.878
Retention policies define how long logs remain useful and how long
sensitive operational data continues to exist.

10
00:00:57.878 --> 00:01:02.556
Retaining everything forever can increase exposure, cost, and legal
complexity.

11
00:01:02.556 --> 00:01:07.635
Deleting too quickly can harm incident response, compliance, and
reliability analysis.

12
00:01:07.635 --> 00:01:15.856
The right retention period depends on business needs, regulatory
expectations, investigation timelines, storage cost, and data sensitivity.

13
00:01:15.856 --> 00:01:20.668
Lifecycle rules should apply to backups and exports, not just the primary
log system.

14
00:01:20.668 --> 00:01:25.680
Privacy-aware logging uses data minimization before access control has to
compensate.

15
00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:35.237
It avoids unnecessary personal data, uses safe identifiers where possible,
limits vendor and support sharing, and treats exported log bundles as
sensitive artifacts.

16
00:01:35.237 --> 00:01:43.324
When logs are shared externally, teams should review what is included,
redact where appropriate, and understand where the data will be stored.

17
00:01:43.324 --> 00:01:47.000
Safe logging does not end when the record leaves the application.
